Third-party components

This repository includes the source code of the third-party libraries Envelock
uses (third_party/, unmodified except as listed in third_party/README.md) and
compiled copies of them (prebuilt/python-wheels/, the official builds):

  * OpenSSL 4.0.3 (https://www.openssl.org/) -- Apache License 2.0.
    Bundled statically in the cryptography wheels. The native loader links
    against the system's OpenSSL 3 (libcrypto) for X25519, HKDF, AES-256-GCM,
    Ed25519 and SHA-256; that library is provided by the server, not shipped.

  * Python "cryptography" 50.0.2 (https://cryptography.io/) -- Apache License
    2.0 or BSD-3-Clause, and the 32 Rust crates it is built from
    (third_party/cryptography-50.0.2/vendor/, each with its own license file:
    MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD-3-Clause, Unicode-3.0 and dual-licensed
    combinations; see third_party/README.md). Used by the encoder and the
    assistant.

  * cffi 2.1.1 (https://cffi.readthedocs.io/) -- MIT-0, including libffi
    (MIT) for Windows.

  * pycparser 3.11 and 3.12 (https://github.com/eliben/pycparser) --
    BSD-3-Clause.

  * nikic/php-parser (https://github.com/nikic/PHP-Parser) -- BSD-3-Clause,
    copyright 2011 Nikita Popov. Source in third_party/php-parser/, used by the
    encoder's AST obfuscator (objective O6). It runs at pack time only and is
    not shipped in the loader.

  * typing_extensions 4.16.0 (https://github.com/python/typing_extensions) --
    Python Software Foundation License 2.0. Needed by cryptography on
    Python 3.10 only.

  * CPython 3.13.15 (https://www.python.org/) -- Python Software Foundation
    License 2.0. Source in third_party/cpython-3.13.15/. The portable Python in
    prebuilt/python/ (official python-build-standalone builds) also bundles
    OpenSSL 3.5.8 (Apache-2.0), SQLite (public domain), zlib, bzip2, xz,
    expat, libffi, mpdecimal, Tcl/Tk, and on Linux/macOS ncurses, libedit,
    libuuid and Berkeley DB 6.0.19 (Sleepycat license; source link in
    prebuilt/README.md), on Linux libX11/libXau/libxcb, on Windows the
    Microsoft Visual C++ runtime, and pip. Their license texts are in
    prebuilt/python/<system>/licenses/; versions and source links in
    prebuilt/README.md.

The assistant executables additionally bundle the Python runtime (Python
Software Foundation License) and these libraries.

Their licenses and notices apply to those components; each one's full license
text is in its folder under third_party/ (libraries) or prebuilt/python/<system>/licenses/
(portable Python).
